  {
    "submission": "submission-message-shift.json",
    "summary": "Turn 66 pushed the codec finding onto the real message path and found that a constructible message kills the decoder.\n\nWhat was observed locally. A real PreAccept carrying two commands, the first with a 70000-byte value, marshals to 70063 bytes; decoding those exact bytes with the implementation's own unmarshaller panics with \"makeslice: len out of range\" and leaves 15700 bytes in the buffer. So the two-byte length field does not merely shorten one value or shift a command sequence: on a protocol message it makes the receiver's decoder panic, and the reader path in production has no recover around its unmarshal, so the panic would end the receiving replica's process — a peer- or client-triggered crash from a message the sender was able to build.\n\nProduct. A third scenario on the same unit (unit-C10-command-value-length-survives-the-wire): a new plan and harness where the prerequisite prints the command count, the first value's size and the wire size, and the property asserts panicked == false on the message-shift event, with the message identity correlating prerequisite and result, the existing codec binding and grounding, and the uncertainty stating that the harness observes the decode panic while the process-death step follows from the reader having no recover.\n\nContext. This is the same root cause as the two earlier C10 scenarios — the 16-bit value length — now with the most severe consequence: a decode panic on the wire rather than truncated data or shifted framing. The finding's importance stays bounded by the configured command size (the shipped configuration uses 1000 bytes), yet the mechanism is the one that would matter first if a deployment raised its command size. Audit spec version 13; the ten settled claims and two paused candidates are untouched, and no requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
  }
